53
00:04:05,326 --> 00:04:09,476
You can use it to store a name, a number, or even something more complex.

54
00:04:09,902 --> 00:04:16,260
For example, you can write name equal to John, which stores the word John as a string in the variable called name.

55
00:04:16,654 --> 00:04:21,284
If you write age equal to 25, the number 25 is stored as an integer.

56
00:04:21,614 --> 00:04:27,300
And similarly, if you write height equal to 5.4, Python understands this as a decimal or a float.

57
00:04:27,726 --> 00:04:32,644
What makes Python really convenient is that you don't need to tell it what kind of data you're storing.

58
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:34,564
It figures that out on its own.

59
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:37,572
This makes writing code much simpler and faster.

60
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,692
Now let's say instead of storing just one piece of data, you want to store a group of items.

61
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:45,120
This is where we use something called a list.

62
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:48,836
A list lets you store multiple values in a single variable.

63
00:04:49,134 --> 00:04:57,092
For example, if you write the information using commas inside a square bracket, you can use indexing starting from 0 to access any value.

64
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:01,680
So if I write person and 0 in the brackets, I will get the output John.

65
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:05,060
Similarly, person 1 will give the output 25.

66
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:07,876
Lists are also mutable or changeable.

67
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:12,160
That means you can update any value from the list using the indexing method.

68
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:20,228
Lists are also flexible, that is, you can add new items to them or remove existing ones using simple commands like append or remove.

69
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:27,492
Now sometimes you might want to store multiple values like in a list, but you want to make sure that no one can change them later.

70
00:05:27,726 --> 00:05:29,092
