So let's start off with supervised learning.

2660
03:20:00,654 --> 03:20:04,868
In supervised learning, we teach the machine using data which is labeled.

2661
03:20:05,262 --> 03:20:09,764
Let's say we have a bunch of fruits and each fruit is tagged with a label.

2662
03:20:10,094 --> 03:20:18,724
So here the machine learns that an apple looks like this, a mango looks like this, a banana looks like this, and an orange looks like this.

2663
03:20:19,086 --> 03:20:24,068
So once the training is done, the machine is fed with new data or test data.

2664
03:20:24,462 --> 03:20:33,284
So here the machine is fed with a new image of Apple and the machine predicts that there is a 97% probability that this is an apple.

2665
03:20:33,710 --> 03:20:38,820
Now supervised learning can again be divided into classification and regression.

2666
03:20:39,246 --> 03:20:43,204
So classifying the input data is a very important task in machine learning.

2667
03:20:43,472 --> 03:20:51,472
For example, whether the mail is genuine or spam, whether the transaction is fraudulent or not, and there are multiple other examples.

2668
03:20:51,854 --> 03:20:58,276
So let's say you live in a gated housing society and your society has separate dust bins for different types of waste.

2669
03:20:58,734 --> 03:21:03,712
So one dust bin for paper waste, one for metal waste, one for plastic waste, and so on.

2670
03:21:03,952 --> 03:21:09,348
So now what you are basically doing over here is classifying the waste into different categories.

2671
03:21:09,678 --> 03:21:15,632
So the classification is the process of assigning a class label to a particular item.

2672
03:21:15,854 --> 03:21:22,788
And in this example, we are assigning the labels paper, metal, plastic, and so on to different types of waste.

2673
03:21:23,470 --> 03:21:30,432
And one thing to note in classification algorithms is that the output variable is categorical in nature.

2674
03:21:30,830 --> 03:21:33,072
Let's take this example to understand it better.

2675
03:21:33,422 --> 03:21:36,900
So over here we have gender of the student and result of the student.

2676
03:21:37,152 --> 03:21:44,672
Here, gender is the input variable or the independent variable, and result is the output variable or the dependent variable.

2677
03:21:44,974 --> 03:21:48,352
So we see that our output variable is categorical in nature.

2678
03:21:48,512 --> 03:21:52,644
That is, it has two categories, either pass or fail.

2679
03:21:53,006 --> 03:21:59,492
And here we are trying to determine whether the student will pass the exam or not on the basis of the gender of the student.

2680
03:22:00,014 --> 03:22:07,588
So there are different classification algorithms, such as decision tree, random forest, naive base, and support vector machine to name a few.

2681
03:22:08,270 --> 03:22:11,236
So let's go through the concept of decision tree briefly.

2682
03:22:11,790 --> 03:22:16,772
Decision tree, as the name states, is a tree-based classifier in machine learning.

2683
03:22:17,102 --> 03:22:24,112
You can consider it to be an upside-down tree where each node splits into its children based on a condition.

2684
03:22:24,494 --> 03:22:27,652
So let's take this example to understand decision tree better.

2685
03:22:28,014 --> 03:22:32,992
Here we are building a decision tree to find out if the person is fit or not.

2686
03:22:33,230 --> 03:22:40,292
And based on a series of test conditions, we finally arrive at the leaf nodes and classify the person to be fit or not.

2687
03:22:40,878 --> 03:22:44,420
So the next type of supervised learning algorithm is regression.

2688
03:22:44,814 --> 03:22:50,980
So when it comes to regression, the output variable or the dependent variable is a continuous numerical.

2689
03:22:51,438 --> 03:22:53,252
So let's take this graph over here.

2690
03:22:53,582 --> 03:22:58,692
Here we have horsepower on the y-axis and miles per gallon on the x axis.

