All right.

8340
09:36:11,968 --> 09:36:19,876
so imbalanced data basically happens when basically when one class in a data set has a lot more samples than the other.

8341
09:36:20,430 --> 09:36:21,616
Now, what does that mean?

8342
09:36:21,856 --> 09:36:24,164
So consider this example over here.

8343
09:36:24,750 --> 09:36:26,336
So this is a balanced data set.

8344
09:36:26,416 --> 09:36:28,096
Now, why is this a balanced data set?

8345
09:36:28,302 --> 09:36:31,856
So we have two classes over here, female and male.

8346
09:36:32,016 --> 09:36:33,156
Let's call them A and B.

8347
09:36:33,248 --> 09:36:34,158
All right.

8348
09:36:34,158 --> 09:36:44,708
Now, class A that is female has 51% percent of samples in a data set, whereas class B, which is male, has 49% percent of samples in the data set.

8349
09:36:45,326 --> 09:36:48,496
Now, this is a pretty uh you can say balanced situation.

8350
09:36:48,576 --> 09:36:48,896
Why?

8351
09:36:48,976 --> 09:36:52,576
Because we have almost almost equal number of samples, right?

8352
09:36:52,814 --> 09:36:55,620
51% percent and 49% percent are not that far away.

8353
09:36:56,270 --> 09:37:05,456
But if you consider this particular scenario, we have 98% of samples in class A, which is our normal okay.

8354
09:37:05,536 --> 09:37:09,892
So this imbalance data is basically for the fraud detection data set.

8355
09:37:14,030 --> 09:37:20,996
Okay, so we have a fraud detection data set where we have two classes, which is normal transactions and fraudulent transactions.

8356
09:37:21,422 --> 09:37:27,296
Now our normal transactions are obviously going to be in a higher ratio compared to a fraudulent transaction.

8357
09:37:27,376 --> 09:37:33,476
So we only have 2% of transactions which were fraud, which is our class B.

