8497
09:48:40,878 --> 09:48:46,020
How do you ensure that the sample chosen for a study truly represents the entire population?

8498
09:48:47,054 --> 09:48:51,588
Now, in order to answer this particular question, first we need to understand what is sampling.

8499
09:48:51,854 --> 09:48:57,604
So whenever we're conducting a study, it is not always going to be possible to include everyone in the population.

8500
09:48:58,000 --> 09:48:58,320
Right?

8501
09:48:58,702 --> 09:49:03,120
So we're going to use sampling to study a smaller, more manageable group.

8502
09:49:03,566 --> 09:49:07,680
So the first step is to define who or what you are studying.

8503
09:49:07,760 --> 09:49:09,920
That is, we need to define the target population.

8504
09:49:10,222 --> 09:49:15,748
So say for example, we want to know the opinions of college students for a particular study.

8505
09:49:16,000 --> 09:49:16,320
Right.

8506
09:49:16,622 --> 09:49:21,604
So our target population is going to be only the college students, not their parents, not the siblings.

8507
09:49:21,920 --> 09:49:22,240
Right?

8508
09:49:22,734 --> 09:49:24,960
So that is how we define the target population.

8509
09:49:25,294 --> 09:49:28,196
The next step would be to choose the right sampling method.

8510
09:49:28,622 --> 09:49:31,680
Now we have over here four different types of sampling methods.

8511
09:49:31,760 --> 09:49:34,020
So the first one is simple random sampling.

8512
09:49:34,400 --> 09:49:39,236
Over here, everyone in the population is going to have an equal chance of being selected.

8513
09:49:39,534 --> 09:49:39,760
Right?

8514
09:49:39,840 --> 09:49:43,012
There is not going to be any particular bias towards any person.

8515
09:49:43,246 --> 09:49:45,380
The next one is systematic sampling.

8516
09:49:45,614 --> 09:49:51,652
So over here, every nth person or nth member from a list or sequence is going to be selected.

8517
09:49:52,014 --> 09:50:02,404
So basically, over here we're going to apply some sort of algorithm or some sort of system that every third person that we meet or every third person that we interview is going to be part of our data set.

8518
09:50:02,702 --> 09:50:03,332
all right?

8519
09:50:03,765 --> 09:50:06,084
Then what we have is a stratified sampling.

8520
09:50:06,510 --> 09:50:13,220
Over here we divide the population into groups, that is our strata, and take a random sample from each group.

8521
09:50:13,265 --> 09:50:14,611
All right?

8522
09:50:14,670 --> 09:50:22,592
so say for example I am interviewing college students, I'm going to divide the college students into groups, say depending on their streams, right?

8523
09:50:22,798 --> 09:50:28,672
So maybe I make groups of computer science and MBA and other fields like that, right?

8524
09:50:28,910 --> 09:50:34,244
So once I have divided the college students into groups, I'm randomly going to pick a student from each group.

8525
09:50:34,670 --> 09:50:37,252
Alright, so this is what is known as stratified sampling.

8526
09:50:37,902 --> 09:50:39,844
Then lastly, we have cluster sampling.

8527
09:50:40,192 --> 09:50:47,716
Over here, we divide the population into clusters, randomly select the clusters, and include all the members from those clusters.

8528
09:50:48,174 --> 09:50:48,512
Alright.

8529
09:50:48,672 --> 09:50:54,244
So over here I'm not going to select an individual from a group, rather, I'm going to select the entire group random.

8530
09:50:54,830 --> 09:50:55,081
All right.

8531
09:50:55,081 --> 09:50:56,932
so this is known as cluster sampling.

8532
09:50:57,232 --> 09:51:01,092
So depending on our problem statement, we need to choose the right sampling method.

8533
09:51:02,158 --> 09:51:05,188
Then, third is to ensure sample size is adequate.

8534
09:51:05,678 --> 09:51:11,012
What this means is larger samples are better because they reduce the likelihood of errors.

8535
09:51:11,392 --> 09:51:18,724
So, say for example, if we are polling 10 people to understand a city's preference, we are going to miss the diversity of opinions.

8536
09:51:19,214 --> 09:51:19,552
Right?

8537
09:51:19,792 --> 09:51:24,484
A sample of thousand is going to be much more reliable and much more adequate.

8538
09:51:25,038 --> 09:51:28,068
So, this is what ensuring the right sample size means.

8539
09:51:28,974 --> 09:51:31,312
Then lastly, we need to avoid bias.

8540
09:51:31,694 --> 09:51:34,912
Bias occurs when certain groups are favored over the others.

8541
09:51:35,246 --> 09:51:40,708
So, say for example, we only survey people from urban areas and we ignore the rural areas.

8542
09:51:41,326 --> 09:51:44,512
So our result is going to be completely skewed, right?

8543
09:51:44,752 --> 09:51:50,032
Obviously, the opinions of urban people are not going to match with the ones of the rural people.

8544
09:51:50,350 --> 09:51:53,952
So this is basically how bias enters the sample.

8545
09:51:54,032 --> 09:51:59,876
So we need to avoid bias in order to have a sample that truly represents the entire population.

