09:54:30,190 --> 09:54:31,792
Okay, moving on to the next question.

8581
09:54:32,032 --> 09:54:33,668
Define confounding variables.

8582
09:54:34,032 --> 09:54:39,908
Now confounding variable is something that influences both the independent and the dependent variables.

8583
09:54:40,526 --> 09:54:51,620
Alright, so confounding variables are often referred to as hidden variables, and they can make a relationship appear between an independent variable and a dependent variable, even if there is none.

8584
09:54:52,398 --> 09:54:55,268
Alright, so this is basically what confounding variables do.

8585
09:54:55,392 --> 09:54:59,460
Now, say for example, you're studying the relationship between exercise and weight loss.

8586
09:55:00,206 --> 09:55:04,036
You find that people who exercise tend to lose more weight.

8587
09:55:04,270 --> 09:55:08,580
But if you don't account for people's diet, it could be the diet causing the weight loss as well.

8588
09:55:08,848 --> 09:55:09,088
Right?

8589
09:55:09,390 --> 09:55:10,788
And not just the exercise.

8590
09:55:11,246 --> 09:55:14,884
So in this case, diet becomes a confounding variable.

8591
09:55:15,246 --> 09:55:15,488
Alright.

8592
09:55:15,568 --> 09:55:18,052
So this is basically how confounding variables work.

8593
09:55:18,894 --> 09:55:22,948
Moving on to the next question: define bias variance trade-off.

8594
09:55:23,662 --> 09:55:23,968
Alright.

8595
09:55:24,238 --> 09:55:28,996
Now, in order to understand bias variance trade-off, we need to understand these terms separately.

8596
09:55:29,358 --> 09:55:35,652
So bias is basically an error due to oversimplistic models that don't capture all patterns in the data.

8597
09:55:36,494 --> 09:55:36,928
Alright.

8598
09:55:37,088 --> 09:55:39,844
So this is an example of high bias in a data.

8599
09:55:40,078 --> 09:55:47,808
So these are our data points, and since we have a high bias in our data, we are not really trying to understand all the data points.

8600
09:55:47,968 --> 09:55:55,876
Rather, due to the simplicity of our model, we are just going to fit a straight line where we assume that most of our data points are being touched.

8601
09:55:56,208 --> 09:55:58,468
But this is a case of underfitting.

8602
09:55:58,688 --> 09:55:59,140
Alright.

8603
09:55:59,470 --> 09:56:01,540
Then the next thing that we have is variance.

8604
09:56:01,728 --> 09:56:06,052
Now this is an error due to models that are too complex and overfit the training data.

8605
09:56:06,368 --> 09:56:08,484
Now this is the opposite of high bias.

8606
09:56:08,768 --> 09:56:16,548
So basically, when we have a high variance or low bias, what we do is we try to learn each and every data point in the data set.

8607
09:56:17,006 --> 09:56:26,244
This leads to overfitting, and this is also a case where the model performs really well on the training data but very poorly on the unseen data.

8608
09:56:26,894 --> 09:56:27,396
Alright.

8609
09:56:27,918 --> 09:56:43,428
So a simple example or simple analogy of a high bias would be a person studying for an exam, and the person is not really trying to understand the definition and the examples of each concept, rather, he just looks at the headings of each concept.

8610
09:56:43,758 --> 09:56:45,892
Right, this would be a case of underfitting.

8611
09:56:46,702 --> 09:57:02,980
But if that same person tries to memorize each and every detail of a particular concept, say he even memorizes the numericals, the exact value of each numerical, the person is going to perform extremely well if the exact same questions appear in the data.

8612
09:57:03,488 --> 09:57:11,748
But if the values of a particular numerical or particular question are changed even slightly, the person is going to fail in that particular case.

8613
09:57:12,398 --> 09:57:14,468
So this is an example of overfitting.

8614
09:57:14,926 --> 09:57:15,328
Alright.

8615
09:57:15,534 --> 09:57:19,684
And overfitting happens when there is a low bias or a high variance.

8616
09:57:22,670 --> 09:57:26,820
And underfitting happens when there is a high bias or low variance.

8617
09:57:28,462 --> 09:57:29,060
Alright?

8618
09:57:29,966 --> 09:57:36,196
Now the entire goal of bias variance trade-off is to find the right balance between bias and variance.

8619
09:57:36,782 --> 09:57:43,268
Now, if we have a low bias and a high variance, our model is going to fit the data well, but it is not going to generalize it.

8620
09:57:44,046 --> 09:57:44,528
Alright?

8621
09:57:45,008 --> 09:57:46,788
Which basically means overfitting.

8622
09:57:49,294 --> 09:57:58,628
But if we have a high bias and low variance, our model is going to be too simple and it is going to miss important patterns or even the general patterns, the basic learnings of the data.

8623
09:57:59,150 --> 09:57:59,568
Alright?

8624
09:57:59,808 --> 09:58:06,884
So the goal is to find a spot where the model is complex enough to capture patterns but simple enough to avoid the overfitting.

8625
09:58:07,182 --> 09:58:07,488
Alright.

8626
09:58:07,598 --> 09:58:09,088
So this is basically what it means.

8627
09:58:09,248 --> 09:58:13,700
This is the kind of point that we are looking for, which gives us the optimal solution.

