10:00:25,454 --> 10:00:30,496
Moving on to the 20th question: what is OOB error and how is it useful?

8655
10:00:30,990 --> 10:00:36,932
OOB error basically stands for out-of-bag error, which is a concept in random forest models.

8656
10:00:37,456 --> 10:00:44,356
Now, this is a way to estimate the model's accuracy using the leftover data that isn't used during the training.

8657
10:00:44,656 --> 10:00:45,136
Alright.

8658
10:00:45,454 --> 10:00:48,416
Now we have an original training data of size 5.

8659
10:00:48,622 --> 10:00:51,216
Now we are going to do sampling with replacement.

8660
10:00:51,296 --> 10:00:58,256
So we have multiple decision trees and we are going to use the sample size of 3 for each particular decision tree.

8661
10:00:58,574 --> 10:01:09,124
Now, OOB error is basically when random forest build multiple decision trees, they train each tree on a random subset of data called a bootstrap sample.

8662
10:01:09,582 --> 10:01:12,804
During this training, some data points are going to be left out.

8663
10:01:12,974 --> 10:01:17,056
Now these are called the out-of-bag data points or OOB error.

8664
10:01:17,390 --> 10:01:17,856
Alright.

8665
10:01:18,094 --> 10:01:26,660
So once the random forest model is trained, the out-of-bag error or the out-of-bag data is going to be used to test the predictions.

8666
10:01:26,926 --> 10:01:31,108
By aggregating the errors from all the trees, we calculate the OOB error.

8667
10:01:31,616 --> 10:01:35,812
This is an estimate of how well the model performs on unseen data.

8668
10:01:36,016 --> 10:01:36,484
Alright.

8669
10:01:36,816 --> 10:01:46,276
So OOB error is useful for internal validation, which basically provides an unbiased accuracy estimate without needing to separate validation data.

8670
10:01:46,670 --> 10:01:48,576
Then it also saves resources.

8671
10:01:48,782 --> 10:01:54,052
So it is going to eliminate the need completely to split the data set into training and testing sets.

8672
10:01:54,286 --> 10:01:54,496
Right?

8673
10:01:54,576 --> 10:02:00,580
Since we are already leaving out some data to be tested on, we do not need to split our data set separately.

8674
10:02:01,102 --> 10:02:03,216
Then it also provides performance check.

8675
10:02:03,470 --> 10:02:07,172
So it is going to help identify if the model is overfitting or underfitting.

8676
10:02:07,456 --> 10:02:08,004
Right.

8677
10:02:08,846 --> 10:02:22,340
So some examples of OB error are suppose a random forest model trains on 70% percent of the data, which is a bootstrap sample, and the remaining 30%, percent which is our out of bag data, is going to be used to test each tree's prediction.

8678
10:02:22,816 --> 10:02:27,460
Now we aggregate these predictions to compute the OOB error in total.

8679
10:02:28,176 --> 10:02:28,736
Alright.

