8726
10:06:08,142 --> 10:06:12,196
Moving on to the 22nd question: what is collaborative filtering?

8727
10:06:12,974 --> 10:06:19,556
So collaborative filtering is basically a method that is used to make recommendations based on user behavior.

8728
10:06:20,032 --> 10:06:23,652
It relies on similarities between the users or the items.

8729
10:06:24,046 --> 10:06:27,072
So collaborative filtering is again divided into two types.

8730
10:06:27,312 --> 10:06:30,730
First is user-based, and second one is item based.

8731
10:06:30,770 --> 10:06:31,371
All right.

8732
10:06:31,371 --> 10:06:38,148
so user-based collaborative filtering is basically when items are suggested based on users with similar preferences.

8733
10:06:38,862 --> 10:06:47,332
Alright, and item based collaborative filtering is basically when the items are suggested similar to what the user has liked previously.

8734
10:06:48,142 --> 10:06:51,076
Alright, let's understand this with the help of an example.

8735
10:06:51,502 --> 10:06:52,836
So there are two users.

8736
10:06:53,776 --> 10:06:55,936
User 1 and user 2.

8737
10:06:56,398 --> 10:06:59,376
So both these users have several movies in common.

8738
10:06:59,456 --> 10:07:03,172
That is, they have both liked these movies or they have highly rated these movies.

8739
10:07:03,536 --> 10:07:13,028
Now, if user 1 has watched a particular movie that user 2 has not, there is a high chance that this particular movie is recommended to user 2.

8740
10:07:13,614 --> 10:07:16,964
Alright, so this is basically how user-based collaborative filtering works.

8741
10:07:17,216 --> 10:07:19,172
Then we have item-based filtering.

8742
10:07:19,822 --> 10:07:22,416
So basically we have three users who go shopping.

8743
10:07:22,638 --> 10:07:28,416
So user 1 has shopped for these particular items, whereas user 2 has shopped for these particular items.

8744
10:07:28,654 --> 10:07:35,428
Now if a third user has shopped for this particular item, this user is going to be recommended items from the similar category.

8745
10:07:35,616 --> 10:07:38,176
Now if this is not clear, let me give you another example.

8746
10:07:38,336 --> 10:07:40,612
So say if you buy a book about machine learning.

8747
10:07:40,750 --> 10:07:47,536
Now what the system is going to do is the system is going to recommend similar books in the same category to you as well.

8748
10:07:47,726 --> 10:07:50,212
Maybe you get recommendations of deep learning as well.

8749
10:07:50,670 --> 10:07:50,976
Right?

8750
10:07:51,056 --> 10:07:53,348
So this is how item-based filtering works.

8751
10:07:53,776 --> 10:07:59,972
Based on your previous preferences, based on your previous likings, you will be recommended items in the future.

8752
10:08:00,750 --> 10:08:01,252
Clear?

8753
10:08:01,536 --> 10:08:05,508
Moving on to the 23rd question: what is a lazy learning algorithm?

8754
10:08:06,158 --> 10:08:14,724
Now, Lazy learning basically refers to algorithms that postpone generalizing from the training data until they receive a query that is a new data point.

8755
10:08:15,598 --> 10:08:20,068
Now they don't process the data during training but instead they store it for you later use.

8756
10:08:20,558 --> 10:08:20,976
Alright?

8757
10:08:21,326 --> 10:08:24,644
So basically they delay generalization data until a query is made.

8758
10:08:25,134 --> 10:08:25,604
Alright.

8759
10:08:26,062 --> 10:08:34,788
So in the training phase, a lazy learning training is almost instant because the algorithm is going to store the data set without building a complex model.

8760
10:08:35,054 --> 10:08:41,796
Whereas in the prediction phase, when a new query comes in, the algorithm is going to compare it to the stored data to make a prediction.

8761
10:08:42,222 --> 10:08:47,008
This step is going to take time because this is going to involve looking through all the stored data.

8762
10:08:47,054 --> 10:08:47,840
all right.

8763
10:08:48,816 --> 10:08:53,860
So one example of lazy learning algorithm is the K nearest neighbors algorithm.

8764
10:08:54,254 --> 10:08:54,678
all right.

8765
10:08:54,678 --> 10:08:59,216
Now what K nearest neighbors does is so say you have five data points about fruits.

8766
10:08:59,470 --> 10:09:04,656
Alright, let's uh say these are apples, some are apples, some are oranges.

8767
10:09:04,736 --> 10:09:06,736
Alright, they're already classified.

8768
10:09:07,726 --> 10:09:08,164
Alright.

8769
10:09:08,654 --> 10:09:13,380
Now say you have a new data point, which is basically a new fruit.

8770
10:09:13,678 --> 10:09:20,004
Now, in order to classify this new fruit, what KNN is going to do is it is going to compare it to the stored data.

8771
10:09:20,176 --> 10:09:23,492
Right, these are your stored data points.

8772
10:09:25,136 --> 10:09:25,456
Right?

8773
10:09:25,616 --> 10:09:27,776
All these are your stored data points.

8774
10:09:27,936 --> 10:09:33,956
Now KNN is going to compare the stored data point and check the K closest neighbors for the new data point.

8775
10:09:34,638 --> 10:09:34,976
Alright.

8776
10:09:35,136 --> 10:09:41,456
So say if most of the neighbors of this new data point are apples, KNN is going to classify it as apples.

8777
10:09:41,536 --> 10:09:43,844
Otherwise, it is going to classify it as oranges.

8778
10:09:44,206 --> 10:09:44,644
Clear?

8779
10:09:44,878 --> 10:09:50,884
So this is how KNN works, and this is basically what uh lazy learning algorithm works.

8780
10:09:51,534 --> 10:09:51,856
Alright.

8781
10:09:52,096 --> 10:09:54,148
Moving on to the 24th question.

8782
10:09:54,416 --> 10:09:58,116
Describe the differences between bagging and boosting algorithms.

8783
10:09:58,570 --> 10:09:59,348
all right.

8784
10:09:59,438 --> 10:10:04,324
So bagging basically works by creating multiple subsets of the training data.

8785
10:10:04,942 --> 10:10:11,076
So bagging and boosting are basically two powerful techniques in machine learning that are used to improve the performance of models.

8786
10:10:11,820 --> 10:10:19,216
All right, So both of these models are basically going to combine multiple models, but the approach and goals are completely different.

8787
10:10:19,374 --> 10:10:19,616
Okay.

8788
10:10:19,822 --> 10:10:21,124
So first let's see bagging.

8789
10:10:21,390 --> 10:10:30,340
Now what bagging does is it is going to create multiple subsets of the same training data and it is going to train independently on each subset.

8790
10:10:31,118 --> 10:10:42,500
All right, So basically bagging works in parallel, whereas boosting trains the model sequentially, where each new model is going to focus on correcting the errors made by the previous model.

8791
10:10:42,926 --> 10:10:43,396
Alright.

8792
10:10:43,886 --> 10:10:47,748
So the final prediction is going to be a weighted combination of all the models.

8793
10:10:48,416 --> 10:10:48,896
Clear?

8794
10:10:49,056 --> 10:10:50,656
The basic difference is clear.

8795
10:10:51,136 --> 10:10:52,656
So let us look at the approach.

8796
10:10:52,750 --> 10:10:58,308
So bagging basically follows a parallel model training, whereas boosting follow a sequential model training.

8797
10:10:58,894 --> 10:11:06,084
The goal of bagging is to reduce variance, whereas boosting has the main goal of reducing bias and variance.

8798
10:11:06,606 --> 10:11:07,044
Alright.

8799
10:11:07,470 --> 10:11:16,356
The basic method of error handling in bagging is to have equal weights for all the models, whereas boosting corrects errors of the previous models.

8800
10:11:16,686 --> 10:11:17,056
Alright.

8801
10:11:17,216 --> 10:11:23,748
So bagging has a lower risk of overfitting, whereas boosting has a higher risk of overfitting if not controlled.

8802
10:11:24,142 --> 10:11:24,644
Alright?

8803
10:11:25,326 --> 10:11:36,256
So basic example of bagging would be random forest where we have multiple decision trees working parallelly on each subset of the training data.

8804
10:11:36,590 --> 10:11:39,524
Whereas boosting algorithms would be ADA boost,

8805
10:11:42,862 --> 10:11:44,100
gradient boosting.

8806
10:11:50,734 --> 10:11:52,272
And XG boost.

8807
10:11:54,958 --> 10:11:55,364
Alright.

8808
10:11:56,462 --> 10:11:58,308
Moving on to the 25th question.

8809
10:11:58,606 --> 10:12:02,148
Explain the central limit theorem and its importance in data science.

8810
10:12:03,214 --> 10:12:12,484
CLT or central limit theorem basically states that the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches a normal distribution as the sample size increases.

8811
10:12:13,134 --> 10:12:13,552
Alright.

8812
10:12:13,742 --> 10:12:21,220
So as you can see over here, as we increase the sample size, the distribution of our data approaches a more normal curve.

8813
10:12:21,870 --> 10:12:22,272
Alright.

8814
10:12:22,432 --> 10:12:28,420
So this basically enables the use of statistical methods that rely only and only on normal distribution.

8815
10:12:28,912 --> 10:12:38,660
It's going to help make inferences about the population using sample data, and it is used in hypothesis testing, confidence intervals, and many more such inferences.

8816
10:12:38,832 --> 10:12:39,204
Alright.

8817
10:12:39,854 --> 10:12:44,356
So basic example would be say you want to measure the average height of students in a school.

8818
10:12:44,846 --> 10:12:49,060
Now the individual heights might vary a lot and may not follow a normal pattern.

8819
10:12:49,326 --> 10:12:49,632
Right?

8820
10:12:49,792 --> 10:13:01,380
But if you repeatedly take random samples of students, calculate the average height for each sample and then plot these averages, the graph will eventually form a normal distribution as the sample grows.

8821
10:13:01,838 --> 10:13:02,308
Alright?

8822
10:13:02,702 --> 10:13:08,836
So the importance of CLT in data science is mainly the foundation for statistical analysis.

8823
10:13:09,072 --> 10:13:14,980
So stats play a major role in data science, and CLT is a basic foundation of stats.

8824
10:13:15,310 --> 10:13:16,526
Right.

8825
10:13:16,526 --> 10:13:24,324
many statistical methods assume normality, and CLT is going to ensure that we can apply these methods even when the original data isn't normal.

8826
10:13:24,718 --> 10:13:25,152
Alright.

8827
10:13:25,326 --> 10:13:27,332
Then comes sampling and efficiency.

8828
10:13:30,222 --> 10:13:30,592
Alright.

8829
10:13:30,752 --> 10:13:37,476
So instead of analyzing the entire population, we can work with smaller and more manageable samples and still get reliable results.

8830
10:13:38,350 --> 10:13:40,484
Finally, we have hypothesis testing.

8831
10:13:43,792 --> 10:13:44,192
Alright.

8832
10:13:44,526 --> 10:13:51,780
So CLT is used to calculate confidence intervals and perform hypothesis tests, which are essential for making data-driven decisions.

8833
10:13:52,142 --> 10:13:54,788
Alright, so this is the importance of CLT in data science.

8834
10:13:55,886 --> 10:14:01,508
Moving on to the 26th question: How do you decide which learning algorithm to use for a given problem?

8835
10:14:02,350 --> 10:14:08,772
Now choosing the right learning algorithm depends on multiple factors, such as the problem type.

8836
10:14:14,190 --> 10:14:16,932
Okay, the size of the data set.

8837
10:14:21,678 --> 10:14:23,620
Then finally, the outcome.

8838
10:14:23,886 --> 10:14:25,472
That is the outcome you desire.

8839
10:14:25,582 --> 10:14:28,228
Now here is how you can make a decision step by step.

8840
10:14:28,512 --> 10:14:28,912
Alright.

8841
10:14:29,198 --> 10:14:39,300
So you need to start by understanding whether you're solving a regression problem, that is, you're predicting continuous values, or is it a classification problem where you have categorical data?

8842
10:14:39,630 --> 10:14:43,076
Or it could be a clustering problem where you have to group similar data.

8843
10:14:43,392 --> 10:14:43,872
Alright.

8844
10:14:44,174 --> 10:14:45,700
Then comes the data set size.

8845
10:14:45,872 --> 10:14:53,476
So if you have a small data set, you can use algorithms like logistic regression or SVM that go better with the smaller data.

8846
10:14:53,934 --> 10:15:01,380
But if you have larger data sets, you need to use algorithms like random forest or neural networks that can handle the complexity and the big scale.

8847
10:15:01,872 --> 10:15:02,308
Right?

8848
10:15:02,990 --> 10:15:04,592
Then comes your computational power.

8849
10:15:04,672 --> 10:15:09,636
So if you have limited resources, you can use simpler models like linear regression or decision trees.

8850
10:15:09,872 --> 10:15:10,112
Right?

8851
10:15:10,286 --> 10:15:13,356
You should not go for something complex like random forest.

8852
10:15:13,356 --> 10:15:19,232
But if you have computational intensive tasks like image recognition, you have to go for neural networks, right?

8853
10:15:19,694 --> 10:15:31,872
Then if you need high accuracy, that is your outcome, if you need a higher accuracy, especially if you have a complex problem, then you need to choose an algorithm like gradient boosting or deep learning, right?

8854
10:15:32,462 --> 10:15:38,308
If your data is very noisy, you need ensemble methods like random forests that can reduce the overfitting.

8855
10:15:38,862 --> 10:15:47,172
And if you have imbalanced data, you need to go for algorithms like SMOT, that is synthetic minority oversampling technique, or you can combine it with SPM.

8856
10:15:47,502 --> 10:15:50,564
Alright, so this is basically how you choose your learning algorithm.

8857
10:15:52,014 --> 10:15:53,936
Moving on to the 27th question.

8858
10:15:54,126 --> 10:15:59,588
Now, this is a sort of scenario-based question which have a very high chance of being asked in an interview.

