8896
10:19:03,438 --> 10:19:06,976
Moving on to the 28th question, which is also a scenario-based question.

8897
10:19:07,182 --> 10:19:10,116
So the question goes, your data set has missing values.

8898
10:19:10,446 --> 10:19:13,060
Now what are the steps that you are going to take to handle them?

8899
10:19:13,296 --> 10:19:19,844
Now, in order to deal with the missing values in a data set, you first need to understand the nature of your missing values.

8900
10:19:20,496 --> 10:19:20,816
Right?

8901
10:19:21,198 --> 10:19:27,776
They could either be missing completely at random or they could be missing at random or they could be missing not at random.

8902
10:19:27,982 --> 10:19:29,444
Now, what do these terms mean?

8903
10:19:29,806 --> 10:19:34,628
So missing completely at random basically means that there is no identifiable pattern.

8904
10:19:38,350 --> 10:19:38,736
Alright.

8905
10:19:39,310 --> 10:19:44,548
Missing at random means that the missing values are somehow correlated with other variables.

8906
10:19:48,494 --> 10:19:53,632
Alright, and missing not at random means that uh they're related to the missing value itself.

8907
10:19:53,902 --> 10:19:59,792
Alright, so say for example, customers are not uh reporting uh low satisfaction scores, alright.

8908
10:20:00,174 --> 10:20:10,916
So if a column consistently has missing values, that is, if a column has more than 50% of missing values, then the best option would be dropping the entire column itself.

8909
10:20:11,406 --> 10:20:11,872
Alright?

8910
10:20:12,462 --> 10:20:19,792
But if that is not the case, that is our missing values do not exceed 50%, the other possible techniques would be ignoring the missing values.

8911
10:20:19,872 --> 10:20:23,364
That is, we drop the particular rows or the particular columns.

8912
10:20:23,790 --> 10:20:26,532
Now, when do we drop the rows or drop the column?

8913
10:20:26,752 --> 10:20:31,872
Whenever we have minimal missing data, that is our missing data is less than 30%.

8914
10:20:32,782 --> 10:20:40,196
Alright, so if a small percentage of rows or columns have missing data, we can consider dropping them without losing the significant information.

8915
10:20:40,686 --> 10:20:41,050
all right.

8916
10:20:41,422 --> 10:20:51,232
So, in order to perform this step, all we have to do is uh let's say our data is stored in DF variable, all we have to do is call drop NA function.

8917
10:20:51,374 --> 10:20:55,364
So this function is basically going to drop all the rows that contain the missing values.

8918
10:20:55,792 --> 10:21:02,500
Now the next technique is imputing missing values, that is, we can replace the missing values with mean, median, or mode.

8919
10:21:02,992 --> 10:21:03,524
Alright.

8920
10:21:04,110 --> 10:21:21,072
So whenever we have a data set that contains outliers, that is, say if we are dealing with a feature like salary, where our salary could range from zero to even crores or even billions, millions, alright.

8921
10:21:21,326 --> 10:21:23,620
There is no end limit to this number, right?

8922
10:21:23,872 --> 10:21:26,672
We could be considering the richest person in India, right?

8923
10:21:26,752 --> 10:21:29,092
We could be considering his or her salary as well.

8924
10:21:29,774 --> 10:21:34,752
So for features like this, we would have outliers, right?

8925
10:21:35,152 --> 10:21:45,284
And if we were to take the mean or the average of this particular feature, our average would not be justified to the lowest and the highest value in this particular feature.

8926
10:21:45,806 --> 10:21:51,872
So in cases like this, we would impute our missing values with something known as median.

8927
10:21:52,272 --> 10:21:58,852
This would give us a more accurate representation or the more accurate imputation of our missing values.

8928
10:21:59,232 --> 10:22:07,140
But if this is not the case, we are dealing with a regular feature, then we can go ahead and impute our missing values with our mean itself.

8929
10:22:07,342 --> 10:22:07,712
Alright.

8930
10:22:07,872 --> 10:22:13,472
And if we are dealing with categorical data, say we are dealing with a feature called fruits, right?

8931
10:22:13,742 --> 10:22:16,752
We have apple, we have orange, and then we have some missing values.

8932
10:22:16,912 --> 10:22:22,724
This is when we will use something known as mode that is the most repeated value in that particular feature.

8933
10:22:23,438 --> 10:22:23,876
Alright.

8934
10:22:25,198 --> 10:22:30,672
So if we have minimal data missing, less than 30%, we just drop the rows or columns.

8935
10:22:30,752 --> 10:22:34,272
If we have substantial data missing, then we impute the data.

8936
10:22:34,574 --> 10:22:39,332
If we have categorical data, we use the mode or unknown for imputation.

8937
10:22:39,758 --> 10:22:43,748
If we have predictive missingness, we create an indicator variable for missingness.

8938
10:22:43,886 --> 10:22:50,660
And if we have mostly the entire column missing, that is more than 50%, we drop the entire column if it is non-critical.

8939
10:22:50,926 --> 10:22:51,332
Alright.

8940
10:22:52,558 --> 10:22:54,432
Moving on with the 29th question.

8941
10:22:54,672 --> 10:22:58,500
An e-commerce website runs two designs for its home page.

8942
10:22:58,702 --> 10:23:01,188
How would you determine which one performs better?

8943
10:23:01,712 --> 10:23:05,572
Now, this is a classic scenario of A B testing.

8944
10:23:05,998 --> 10:23:06,432
Alright.

8945
10:23:06,766 --> 10:23:11,312
A B testing is basically a method that compares two versions A and B.

8946
10:23:11,392 --> 10:23:17,124
It could be of a website or any other scenario to determine which one performs better.

8947
10:23:17,646 --> 10:23:18,112
Alright.

8948
10:23:18,272 --> 10:23:19,392
Now, how does it work?

8949
10:23:19,662 --> 10:23:24,676
So A B testing is basically going to split the website visitors randomly into two groups.

8950
10:23:25,102 --> 10:23:30,148
So group A is going to see design 1 and group B is only going to see design 2.

8951
10:23:30,958 --> 10:23:31,392
Alright.

8952
10:23:31,886 --> 10:23:40,032
So after the groups have seen the two designs, we are then going to measure the performance of both designs using a key metric like conversion rate.

8953
10:23:40,112 --> 10:23:42,660
That is the percentage of users making a purchase.

8954
10:23:43,150 --> 10:23:43,472
Alright.

8955
10:23:43,790 --> 10:23:47,780
So we are going to separately calculate the conversion rate for group 1 and group 2.

8956
10:23:48,078 --> 10:23:55,428
So whichever conversion rate is higher, we are then going to classify as that particular website page as more efficient than the other.

8957
10:23:55,758 --> 10:23:56,192
Alright.

8958
10:23:56,352 --> 10:23:57,872
So you need to define the goal.

8959
10:23:57,952 --> 10:24:02,512
You need to identify the key performance metrics such as the click-through rate, purchases, and so on.

8960
10:24:02,672 --> 10:24:02,832
Right?

8961
10:24:02,912 --> 10:24:06,884
It could be n number of factors depending on what kind of website you are building.

8962
10:24:07,232 --> 10:24:08,912
Then you need to split the traffic.

8963
10:24:09,072 --> 10:24:12,100
So you need to randomly divide the users into equal groups.

8964
10:24:12,512 --> 10:24:12,832
Right?

8965
10:24:13,198 --> 10:24:18,192
Then you run the experiment, that is, you show each group their respective design for a set duration.

8966
10:24:18,382 --> 10:24:18,752
Alright.

8967
10:24:18,832 --> 10:24:20,512
Then lastly, you analyze the results.

8968
10:24:20,592 --> 10:24:29,252
That is, you compare the metrics, that is, the conversion rates, the click through rate, or whatever other performance metric you have decided to determine the better design.

8969
10:24:29,998 --> 10:24:30,436
Alright.

8970
10:24:30,798 --> 10:24:35,364
So this is how you determine which one of the two versions you have made performs better.

8971
10:24:35,758 --> 10:24:36,196
Alright.

8972
10:24:37,038 --> 10:24:39,936
Moving on to the last question for this particular video.

8973
10:24:40,176 --> 10:24:44,324
Your data set has 50 columns, but not all are useful.

8974
10:24:44,718 --> 10:24:46,692
How would you choose the important ones?

8975
10:24:47,630 --> 10:24:48,096
Alright.

8976
10:24:48,256 --> 10:24:51,204
So this is a classic example of feature selection.

8977
10:24:51,630 --> 10:24:58,884
So feature selection basically involves removing the irrelevant or redundant data, which is the main objective of our question over here.

8978
10:24:59,136 --> 10:24:59,376
Right?

8979
10:24:59,456 --> 10:25:04,736
Out of the 50 columns, we need to keep only the relevant ones and remove the rest.

8980
10:25:05,838 --> 10:25:11,972
Feature selection basically helps improve the model accuracy and performance and it also reduces the computation time.

8981
10:25:12,270 --> 10:25:12,510
Right?

8982
10:25:12,846 --> 10:25:15,940
So say for example, out of 50 columns, only 10 are useful.

8983
10:25:16,782 --> 10:25:19,908
This is going to reduce the data size significantly.

8984
10:25:20,336 --> 10:25:20,708
Right?

8985
10:25:21,550 --> 10:25:25,476
So the basic methods for feature selection are correlation analysis.

8986
10:25:25,742 --> 10:25:26,038
right?

8987
10:25:26,038 --> 10:25:30,308
You need to identify the highly correlated columns and remove the redundant ones.

8988
10:25:30,503 --> 10:25:31,240
right?

8989
10:25:31,456 --> 10:25:37,188
So basically, if two columns are highly correlated, one of them may be redundant or basically useless.

8990
10:25:37,518 --> 10:25:44,484
So say for example, if age and years of experience are strongly correlated, you can keep either one of these.

8991
10:25:45,358 --> 10:25:51,588
You can also use correlation heat maps in Python using the seaborn library and the heat map function.

8992
10:25:52,238 --> 10:25:54,724
Then comes feature importance using models.

8993
10:25:55,022 --> 10:26:08,164
So you can use models like random forest or gradient boosting rank features by importance, So say for example, in predicting the house prices, your location feature might rank higher than paint color.

8994
10:26:08,496 --> 10:26:08,896
Alright.

8995
10:26:09,262 --> 10:26:15,776
So there's a high probability that removing the paint color is just going to make your model better.

8996
10:26:16,270 --> 10:26:16,708
Right?

8997
10:26:17,422 --> 10:26:23,776
Then comes statistical methods, so you can apply methods like ANOVA or Chi-Square to assess the feature significance.

8998
10:26:24,302 --> 10:26:27,044
Then finally comes dimensionality reduction.

8999
10:26:27,342 --> 10:26:38,468
So you can use techniques like PCA, principal component analysis, which as we discussed use eigenvalues and eigenvectors to reduce the features while retaining the key information.

